# Regulated / High-Assurance Overlay — <%= projectName %>

> **Overlay:** `industryOverlay: regulated` · **Governance:** <%= governanceLevel %>
> **Gate:** `scripts/check-regulated-overlay.mjs` (fail-closed)
> **Policy:** `.arbiter/regulated/overlay.json`

This overlay bundles the controls a high-assurance project is expected to keep
wired and makes the **floor** machine-enforceable. The gate fails closed: if the
policy manifest is missing, unparseable, or any pillar is weakened below the
floor, the gate exits non-zero and blocks the release. You may **tighten** any
value above the floor; you may not drop below it while claiming `regulated`.

## Pillars

| Pillar | Floor (enforced) | Why |
| ------ | ---------------- | --- |
| **Separation of duties** | An AI-authored PR requires `>= 1` independent human approval; AI authorship is recognised via configured markers. | No actor both authors and approves a change — the bedrock of high-assurance change control. Composes with the `ai-draft-check` workflow. |
| **Audit-trail retention** | A declared retention horizon `>= 365` days. | A change must remain reconstructable long after it merges. |
| **Suppression expiry** | Every suppression is mandatory-expiring and bounded (`maxValidityDays`). | A waiver is a dated exception, never a permanent hole — enforced by `scripts/check-suppressions.mjs`. |
| **Signing + SBOM** | Release artefacts are cosign-signed and ship an SBOM. | Provenance and supply-chain integrity are attestable, not assumed. |
| **Mutation coverage** | A minimum mutation score floor. | Tests must kill real mutants — coverage that does not catch defects is theatre. |

## How it is enforced

1. `.arbiter/regulated/overlay.json` declares the policy (the floor + any tightening).
2. `scripts/check-regulated-overlay.mjs` validates every pillar against the floor.
3. The generated `check-all.mjs` runs the gate (present only when this overlay is selected).

## Editing the policy

Open `.arbiter/regulated/overlay.json` and raise any value — for example a longer
retention horizon, a higher mutation floor, or `minHumanApprovals: 2` for
four-eyes-plus review. Lowering a value below the regulated floor is a gate
violation by design.

> This overlay is **opt-in** and de-identified: it expresses high-assurance
> controls in neutral terms, with no domain-, sector-, or regulation-specific
